To deploy this code as a Lambda function, you'll also need to create a `lambda_function_handler.go` file with the following code:

```go
package main

import (
    "github.com/aws/aws-lambda-go/lambda"
)

func handler(ctx context.Context) (interface{}, error) {
    return uploadFileToS3, nil
}

func uploadFileToS3(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) error {
    return s3UploadFileToS3(r)
}

func s3UploadFileToS3(r *http.Request) error {
    // Get the file from the request
    file, header, err := r.FormFile("file")
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }

    // Upload the file to S3
    _, err = s3Client.PutObject(&s3.PutObjectInput{
        Bucket: aws.String("your-bucket"),
        Key:    aws.String("uploads/" + header.Filename),
        Body:   io.NopCloser(file),
    })
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }

    // Return a success response
    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)
    w.Write([]byte("File uploaded successfully"))
    return nil
}
```

You'll also need to create a `package.json` file with the following code:

```json
{
  "name": "lambda-function",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "scripts": {
    "build": "go build -o main main.go",
    "zip": "zip -r lambda_function.zip main",
    "upload": "aws lambda update-function-code --function-name lambda-function --zip-file file://lambda_function.zip"
  }
}
```

You can then build and upload your code as follows:

```bash
npm run build
npm run zip
aws lambda update-function-code --function-name lambda-function --zip-file file://lambda_function.zip
```

This will create a new Lambda function with the specified code and configuration. You can then test the function using the AWS Lambda console or the AWS CLI.

